<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mustafa Chokay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mustafa Shokay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mustafa Chokay was a Kazakh lawyer and politician who headed the Turkestan Autonomy, proclaimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> in 1917 within a hoped-for democratic Russian federation. When the Bolsheviks destroyed Kokand in February 1918 he escaped, eventually settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he edited émigré journals and campaigned for Turkestani independence until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-chokay.jpg" alt="Mustafa Chokay" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D. V. Nazarov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mustafa_Shokay_in_youth_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
